WEBVTT

00:00:19.060 --> 00:00:23.260
AI Company Employees Should Unionize. Author not named.

00:00:23.910 --> 00:00:36.760
Part one. Should safety-conscious employees at AI companies quit in protest, or stay and fight? The latest argument didn't resolve the dispute, but it did get me in contact with organizers working on both sides.

00:00:37.410 --> 00:00:55.585
Those planning to stay and fight are increasingly interested in labor organization. This doesn't need to mean literal government-recognized Labor Unions. An existing group, Coalition Of Concerned AI Staff, is already helping support interested employees and investigating other models to do more.

00:00:55.585 --> 00:01:03.310
But it might involve using laws, rights, and tactics associated with the National Labor Relations Act and the labor movement.

00:01:03.960 --> 00:01:21.085
You may remember stories of bitter unionization battles at places like Starbucks, but AI workers can probably route around that. The toughest labor disputes happen in fields where the employer could easily fire all the organized workers and replace them from a bottomless pool of faceless scabs.

00:01:21.085 --> 00:01:41.610
Although this is illegal, everyone needs to be maximally lawyered-up to prevent it. But there are so few skilled AI researchers that these laws are hardly necessary. If a dozen top AI researchers threaten to walk, it doesn't matter whether Section P Sub-Section Q forces the AI company to negotiate. The AI company will want to negotiate.

00:01:41.610 --> 00:01:57.485
This cuts through the red tape that intimidates workers in less blessed fields. AI researchers could likely get away with only the package of rights provided by informal "concerted activity", defined as two or more workers doing anything vaguely labor-activism-shaped.

00:01:57.485 --> 00:02:05.210
Just put up a sign somewhere saying "OpenAI Safety Union" and get at least two employees to stand behind it, and it counts.

00:02:05.860 --> 00:02:14.960
Even the word "union" is unnecessary. If employees felt like it was too adversarial, they could call it "OpenAI Research Council" or something.

00:02:15.610 --> 00:02:30.735
Once an organization existed, the law would ban the company from firing people simply for being part of it, or talking to other members, or planning demands or actions together. It also couldn't fire workers for striking over workplace conditions.

00:02:31.385 --> 00:02:50.285
Does the "workplace conditions" requirement rule out most forms of safety activism? Not necessarily. Many safety issues double as workplace issues, including whistleblower protections, auditing requirements, and safety team staffing. Most of all, fears of recursive self-improvement are naturally workplace-related.

00:02:50.285 --> 00:03:01.135
Whatever else R S I does to the world, it will also automate AI researcher positions. This would be a popular starting issue that might attract even non-alignment-pilled colleagues.

00:03:01.785 --> 00:03:20.310
But even if some safety issue has no workplace relevance at all, employees can still strike over it. They only lose their protection against being fired. And good AI researchers are worth tens of millions of dollars each and nearly irreplaceable, so AI companies are already averse to firing dozens of them at once.

00:03:20.310 --> 00:03:38.485
And any attempt to fire striking AI researchers would likely make national news and be a P R disaster. So the legal protection against being fired isn't doing most of the work anyway, and AI employee organizations could strike over issues without direct workplace relevance if they wanted.

00:03:39.135 --> 00:03:49.235
What if employees think their company already does a good job prioritizing safety? Then they should organize in order to give their already-virtuous company more options.

00:03:49.235 --> 00:04:01.935
Negotiations between unions get less antitrust scrutiny than negotiations between companies, so workers can forge deals to slow research or prioritize safety even when executives' hands are tied.

00:04:02.585 --> 00:04:15.860
For example, suppose that OpenAI leadership orders its researchers to switch to a less-monitorable looped transformer design. A safety-conscious employee union might ask further questions, or mutually refuse to pursue this.

00:04:15.860 --> 00:04:31.110
If Sam Altman said that they had to build a looped transformer, or else Anthropic would build it first and beat them, the organizers could call up their friends at the Anthropic Employee Union and ask whether they would go on strike if Anthropic built a looped transformer.

00:04:31.110 --> 00:04:41.335
If the Anthropic Employee Union said yes, then the two unions could present a united front, and their companies wouldn't have to worry about the other company racing ahead.

00:04:41.985 --> 00:04:55.935
Although on its own this has limited value, since Meta or DeepSeek could still build the looped transformer, these union deals could also provide a standard that the industry could rally around, or a clear starting point for government intervention.

00:04:55.935 --> 00:05:06.985
In an extreme case, two unions could negotiate the beginnings of a pause deal, force it on their parent companies, and the government could take note and make it part of a future international deal.

00:05:11.595 --> 00:05:16.145
What else could unions or union-like organizations work toward?

00:05:16.795 --> 00:05:31.420
First, upholding normal protections around discipline and firing. In October, OpenAI fired three safety researchers after accusing them of leaking information on the company's experiments with potentially-dangerous looped transformers.

00:05:31.420 --> 00:05:49.320
These accusations now seem to be false, with the most likely scenario being that OpenAI was angry about the leak and fired some people in the vicinity of the problem to make a point. If OpenAI employees had a union, the company might have to be more careful about firing employees under false pretenses.

00:05:49.970 --> 00:06:03.895
Second, holding companies to the commitments they made during September's excitement around Pacing The Frontier. For example, demanding that they hire independent evaluators with employee-level access by such-and-such a time.

00:06:04.545 --> 00:06:16.145
Third, demanding transparency. For example, about compute allocation as described in an earlier piece, or about the information necessary to measure the speed of recursive self-improvement.

00:06:16.795 --> 00:06:34.445
Fourth, acting as an independent voice holding companies to their commitments. This is another one that works even if, and perhaps especially if, employees already trust their company leadership. Currently, companies struggle to make credible promises because there's nobody inside who can enforce them.

00:06:34.445 --> 00:06:42.320
A union that threatened to investigate or raise a fuss if a promise were broken could help executives be taken seriously.

00:06:42.970 --> 00:06:53.495
If you're interested, contact the people taking point on this, who I can vouch for as smart and trustworthy. The original gives an email address for them, which I have left out here.

00:06:58.105 --> 00:07:09.155
Part two. For people taking the other route, whistleblowing or quitting, my contacts recommend talking to Psst, a nonprofit that provides legal advice for concerned tech workers.

00:07:09.805 --> 00:07:21.780
Whistleblowing is a fraught process. Your company has more experience catching and neutralizing whistleblowers than you have being one, and if you're working on your own, then you will probably fail to cover your tracks.

00:07:21.780 --> 00:07:33.880
Did you know companies look to see how many times different employees checked leaked documents, on the grounds that someone who's agonizing about whether to whistleblow about them probably reads them more often than most?

00:07:33.880 --> 00:07:48.330
Another classic mistake is to complain about something internally, then whistleblow after your complaints are not taken seriously. It's not hard for companies to connect the dots and realize that the complainer and the whistleblower are probably the same person.

00:07:48.330 --> 00:07:54.005
Psst has more experience with this than you or your company, and can walk you through the process.

00:07:54.655 --> 00:08:11.180
Even if you're thinking of quitting, Psst may be able to help. You may have more options than you thought, like threatening to quit unless you get the thing you want. And if you do decide to quit, you may want to do it in the most effective way possible. Make a splash on your way out, like Jacob Coxon did.

00:08:11.180 --> 00:08:17.230
This requires some basic media literacy, and Psst will connect you with the people who can give it to you.

00:08:17.880 --> 00:08:30.580
Contact Psst by messaging at Psst Legal dot zero one on Signal, on a personal device without any employer spyware. The person on the other end will be a lawyer, and your conversation with them will be confidential.

00:08:31.230 --> 00:08:41.355
Whatever AI employees do, they should do it soon. R S I could start making them obsolete as soon as next year, so they may never have this amount of leverage again.

00:08:45.965 --> 00:08:49.390
The piece ends with notes, which I'll read in order.

00:08:50.040 --> 00:09:04.265
On the point about red tape in other fields: to become an official majority union, a union must win the support of a majority of workers in a secret ballot. This gives them a few extra rights, like forcing companies to bargain with them.

00:09:04.265 --> 00:09:22.915
The companies don't have to agree to any bargain, but can get in trouble for bargaining in bad faith if they're pointlessly recalcitrant. But it also restricts the union's ability to legally advocate for non-workplace-related causes. A union can also get some of these rights by having an employer voluntarily recognize them.

00:09:22.915 --> 00:09:27.765
We don't expect that AI company unions will go down either of these paths.

00:09:28.415 --> 00:09:44.515
On the point about striking: companies do have the option of replacing striking workers during a strike, then firing them after the strike because their jobs are already filled. But frontier AI researchers are hard to replace en masse, so they may not need to worry about this.

00:09:45.165 --> 00:10:04.340
On workplace relevance: if AI destroyed the world, wouldn't that negatively impact working conditions at AI companies? Legal theory only considers a generally destructive event detrimental to working conditions if it would damage the conditions more than it damaged everything else, so this argument probably wouldn't hold.

00:10:04.990 --> 00:10:24.115
On the looped transformer example: this particular example may straddle the border of legality. The situation might change depending on how much it seemed like a nod-and-wink deal between Sam and Dario, and whether it was a purely political demand, as in this case, versus having a plausible connection to working conditions.

00:10:24.115 --> 00:10:37.840
I would still be excited about borderline-legal examples, because court cases might last longer than my timeline to A G I anyway, the publicity would be pure positive, and any sanctions if it turned out illegal would likely be weak.

00:10:38.490 --> 00:10:56.715
And finally: legally recognized unions get more antitrust exemptions and bargaining rights, and potentially could bring both Anthropic and OpenAI to the table and hammer something out. But they are under closer scrutiny to ensure that they focus on workplace conditions rather than issues of general interest.
